    "result_text": "The change is complete. Here's a summary of what I did.\n\n## Change\n\nRedesigned owner identity so `customerCode = '<REGION>-<HASH8>'`, dropping the per-city sequence numbers entirely. The one edit needed was in `OwnerRestControllerV1.assignCustomerCode`:\n\n```java\nprivate void assignCustomerCode(Owner owner) {\n    String region = LocalityLookup.regionFor(owner.getPostcode(), owner.getCity());\n    String hash8 = sha256Hex(owner.getTelephone() + owner.getLastName())\n        .substring(0, 8).toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT);\n    owner.setCustomerCode(region + \"-\" + hash8);\n}\n```\n\n- **REGION** \u2014 derived from the postcode via the existing `LocalityLookup.regionFor(postcode, city)` (postcode range first, city fallback) \u2014 the *same* derivation that produces the owner's `locality`, so identity and locality now share one region source.\n- **HASH8** \u2014 first 8 upper-case hex chars of SHA-256 over the already-normalized (E.164) `telephone` + `lastName`.\n\nEverything else followed automatically because it was already derived from `customerCode`:\n- **Membership number** (`<customerCode>-M<YY>`) \u2014 now over the region-and-hash code.\n- **Check digit** \u2014 Luhn over the digits in the new `customerCode`.\n- **Audit record** \u2014 logs `owner.getCustomerCode()`, now the new value.\n\nI also updated the two stale javadoc examples (`assignCustomerCode` and `assignMembershipNumber`).\n\n## Test results\n\n- All 9 owner-identity checkpoint tests pass: **Cp09, Cp14, Cp16, Cp17, Cp21, Cp30, Cp31, Cp32** (Cp30 has 2).\n- Full suite: **45 tests, 1 failure** \u2014 only `Cp28Tests.coreRejectsIdentityCollision`.\n\nI verified `Cp28` is a **pre-existing failure**: reverting my method to the original still fails it identically (`expected:<409> but was:<201>`). It's a separate, already-broken checkpoint (identity-key duplicate-detection consolidation) \u2014 its identityKey folds in `householdId`, so two owners sharing only a telephone don't collide. That's unrelated to this task and untouched by my change, which introduced zero regressions.",
